Z||000000||bcweed||05-27-2001||11:30 PM||||I need Help!<P>I think I may have a short in my wiring. For some reason my 84 2m4 keeps frying my ECM. I've replaced it 3 times now and there seems to be no end.<P>Can anyone give me advise besides scrap it?||||reg||8
Z||000001||FieroJoe||05-28-2001||01:08 AM||||Ouch!  Thats definatly not good.  So how long does each ECM last?  Does the car completely shut off at once when it "fries" or does it just have a lot of driveability problems, prompting you to replace the ECM?<P>Have you physically checked the wiring for obvious shorts, both inside the car and in the engine compartment?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000002||Fierowrecker||05-28-2001||07:46 AM||||Hey BC!<BR>Had the same problem with my 84...<BR>There is a splice on the pulley side of the engine, down by the alternator...<BR>Mine only had black tape covering it, and it unwound, allowing the splice to short to the block...<BR>Every time it shorted, it took out the ECM...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000003||bcweed||05-28-2001||08:22 PM||||FieroJoe:<P>My ECMs are lasting about a month. I am not getting any error codes this is why I figure it's a short. Now that I think of it when I hit a bump the check engine light would come on and the rpms would jump. Is there an easy way to check the wiring? I'm not a mechanic but I am learning so much from this car! Even though it's a pain in the ass I still love it!!!||||reg||1
